WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.262
A wind turbine, a bridge, a jet engine.

2
00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:08.857
Imagine each had a living copy inside a computer, obeying the same physics.

3
00:00:09.357 --> 00:00:16.964
That copy is a digital twin: a virtual model of one specific physical thing, linked to it by data throughout its life.

4
00:00:16.974 --> 00:00:21.143
It has three parts: the object, the model, and the connection.

5
00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:25.254
If data moves by hand, it is only a digital model.

6
00:00:25.264 --> 00:00:30.364
If data flows automatically from object to model, it is a digital shadow.

7
00:00:30.374 --> 00:00:35.683
It becomes a twin when information also flows back and acts on the object.

8
00:00:36.283 --> 00:00:39.074
So how is that different from a simulation?

9
00:00:39.084 --> 00:00:42.193
A simulation is an experiment you run and close.

10
00:00:42.203 --> 00:00:48.260
A twin persists, with one identity and one history, checked against reality again and again.

11
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.024
Even before anything is built, the twin can work.

12
00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:58.371
Its first data are the laws of nature: gravity, the stiffness of steel, the conservation of energy.

13
00:00:58.381 --> 00:01:04.421
A solver steps these equations through time, so engineers can ask: what if the load doubles?

14
00:01:05.021 --> 00:01:06.291
Now the lifecycle.

15
00:01:06.301 --> 00:01:09.794
Phase zero: the prototype twin designs the product.

16
00:01:09.804 --> 00:01:12.556
Phase one: the digital thread builds it.

17
00:01:12.566 --> 00:01:16.329
Phase two: the instance twin runs it with live sensors.

18
00:01:16.339 --> 00:01:20.034
Phase three: the aggregate twin learns from the fleet.

19
00:01:20.044 --> 00:01:24.421
Phase four: it retires, and its lessons shape the next design.

20
00:01:25.021 --> 00:01:28.210
Physics gives the twin its first life.

21
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.014
Data keeps it honest.

22
00:01:30.024 --> 00:01:32.521
Now explore it for yourself.
